    "text": "A&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>750</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Hz</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mfenced><mi>rms</mi></mfenced></math> source is connected to a resistance of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi><mo>,</mo></math> an inductance of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1803</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math> and a capacitance of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> all in series. The time in which the resistance (heat capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>/</mo><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> ) will get heated by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>.</mo></math> (assume no loss of heat to the surroundings) is close to :"
  },
